WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.140
Let's give it up for this
amazing crowd tonight.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.060
What's up, Boston?

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.844
Woo-hoo!

00:00:19.844 --> 00:00:22.334
[CHEERING]

00:00:22.334 --> 00:00:24.824
(SINGING) Do what I
want 'cause I can,

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:27.314
if I don't, because I wanna.

00:00:27.314 --> 00:00:29.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.420
We have four bands
competing tonight.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.360
They each get a
15-minute set, and then

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.360
at the end of the night, the
judging panel comes together

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:38.500
and decides on a winner.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.380
Good job.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.120
Sounded great.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.390
That winner gets 50%
of the charity proceeds

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:44.740
for their charity of choice.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.213
(SINGING) We will,
we will rock you.

00:00:49.213 --> 00:00:51.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.490
The idea behind the Battle
of the Biotech Bands

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.700
has to do with doing
what we can for children

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.410
and their families who
are fighting diseases,

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:04.660
here and around the world.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:09.490
(SINGING) Don't you
forget about me.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.990
We know there are
patients out there

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:15.735
who are in dire need of serious
treatment for serious diseases.

00:01:15.735 --> 00:01:17.110
And so any little
bit that we can

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.780
do, whether it's through our
work or through music, passions

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.900
that we have, we just want
to come out and rock it.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.150
(SINGING) Aaaaah, ah,
ah-ah-ah-ah, yeah.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.680
The creativity here
tonight is an example

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.590
of the creativity that
goes on in the workplace

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.320
and in those laboratories.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.010
If you guys do your science
as well as you do your music,

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.250
we've got nothing
to worry about here.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.540
[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:48.110
In the Boston area,
biotechnology literally

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:50.480
takes center stage.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.330
And you don't have to
look far to see why.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.480
That's because the region
is home to the biotechnology

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.720
capital of the
world, Kendall Square

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.600
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.300
Here, in less than
one square mile,

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.490
clustered around the
Massachusetts Institute

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.560
of Technology, are more than
100 biotechnology companies,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.670
nearly all of the world's
top pharmaceutical firms,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:21.830
and millions of square
feet of lab space.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.050
Groundbreaking
drugs developed here

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.610
have already touched the lives
of 2 billion people worldwide,

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.580
and more treatments
are in the pipeline.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.780
Kendall Square is simply
the densest collection

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.290
of life scientists in
the known universe.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.920
We're making big
inroads in cancer.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.400
We'll make big inroads
in Alzheimer's.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.120
We'll make big inroads in
ALS, and many other diseases.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:53.750
But just a few decades ago,
this industry and the science

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.600
underlying it didn't exist.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.970
Kendall Square was
a desolate landscape

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:03.890
of shuttered manufacturing
plants and vacant lots.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.470
When I arrived here in
1960, Kendall Square

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.820
looked a bit like a war zone.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.940
It was an area
that was deserted.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.090
And beyond getting on and
off at the train station,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.340
people typically
didn't go there.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.100
And now, when you
walk down the street,

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:22.850
there are young,
excited people who

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:27.590
are racing to get someplace,
busy at some piece of inventing

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.170
the future.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:29.930
It's dynamic.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:30.760
It's energetic.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.700
It's vibrant.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.370
It's innovative.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:34.280
It's dense.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.440
You bump into people
a lot, which I think

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.800
is the beauty of the place.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.150
So what brought about
this dramatic change?

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.770
How did this
industry that affects

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:49.070
so many lives and drives the
economy of an entire region

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.480
arise in such a
short period of time?

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.800
And why here, in such an
unlikely-seeming area?

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.680
I hesitate to say that
there was a logic to it.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.900
It was not a deliberate path
that led us to where we are.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.280
There was a lot of courage.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.250
People really were willing
to follow the science,

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.240
take risks, to dare to do things
that otherwise are completely

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.538
unthinkable.

00:04:14.538 --> 00:04:18.522
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:36.410
They drove me to Rockefeller
on this day in July 1962.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.800
Eventually, the person
living in the next room

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.940
introduced himself.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.310
He was a hippie.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:49.340
He had long hair, white
clothes, sandals, unshaven.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.510
My mother peeked into the room,
and it was a bit of a mess.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.910
And her parting
words to me were,

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.980
Harvey, he's not our kind, I
don't want you to have anything

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.660
to do with him.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.960
However, 13 years later, when
David Baltimore won the Nobel

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:10.490
Prize, it was all over
Cleveland, my son's best friend

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.860
just won the Nobel Prize.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.410
When I came here in
1960, we were just

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:22.880
starting molecular biology
we had no reputation

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:24.830
as a school of biology.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:30.040
No one even knew
that MIT did biology.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.844
Today, biology permeates
every part of MIT.

00:05:34.844 --> 00:05:38.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.680
Biology meant everything.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.130
It meant the kingdoms
and the phyla

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:46.080
and it meant physiology
and strange creatures.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.840
[INSECTS CHIRPING]

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.590
Until that time, biology was a
collection of unrelated facts

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.750
and observations,
rather than a discipline

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.890
governed by a small set
of unifying principles.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.070
Watson and Crick discovered
the structure of DNA

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:07.170
in 1953 with the data
from Rosalind Franklin.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:12.450
MIT's leadership recognized
that this was the unifying

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.720
concept for biology.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.310
MIT said, we just
can't do everything.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:21.390
We're going to focus just
on molecular biology.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:26.910
Molecular biology was a view
of biology as an information

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:27.950
science.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.500
And the information
was encoded in DNA.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:36.600
It's just about then that
Salvador Luria, a refugee

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.560
from Italy, came to MIT.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.640
Salvador Luria, who the next
year won the Nobel Prize,

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.380
was really the heart and soul
of the MIT biology department.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.130
Luria had a vision
that all of biology

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:57.540
was going to be transformed
by a molecular approach

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.840
to questions about
living systems.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.540
And what happened
in the early 1960s

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.488
was this brilliant flash of
insight on the part of many

00:07:06.488 --> 00:07:08.280
that one could really
understand everything

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.490
if one really understood the
details of how cells were

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.820
organized, how they turned
on and off different genes

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:17.080
and proteins.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.410
The year was 1963.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.000
The genetic code was
still being cracked.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.920
Jim Watson would
come to class talking

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.070
about DNA and this
revolution which

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.440
is called molecular biology.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.420
And I'm listening, and
at the end of the hour,

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:34.600
I'm a different person.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:36.850
My life has been redirected.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:40.240
And it was clear that he
was literally telling us

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.770
the secret of life, and how
everything biological gets made

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.650
and why it works
the way it does.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:51.430
With this scheme of DNA
makes RNA makes proteins,

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.920
one could explain
the entire biosphere

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.310
with a very simple
conceptual structure.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.130
What molecular
biology had given us

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.810
was the ability to study
the molecules that determine

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.670
the properties of life.

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:05.170
I thought one day,
people will be

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:08.180
able to work on cancer
at the molecular level.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:10.570
They'll be able to understand
how cancer comes about

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:11.890
by studying genes.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:15.940
More people each year die of
cancer in the United States

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:20.200
than all the Americans who lost
their lives in World War II.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.140
Richard Nixon was elected
president in 1968.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:26.570
And he's very concerned about
being re-elected in 1972.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:29.799
And so Nixon says he will
declare a war on cancer,

00:08:29.799 --> 00:08:34.570
and push for legislation in
1971 as a way of preempting this

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:38.140
as an issue in the
presidential election.

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.960
This funding by Nixon
was critically important

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.360
because Luria
correctly perceived

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.130
that the war on
cancer would offer him

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.370
some largesse in building
up the MIT Cancer Center

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:50.650
and in recruiting faculty.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.020
We built the Cancer
Center in an old candy

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.330
factory, which MIT owned.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:02.680
It was a very audacious
idea that molecular biology

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.060
was going to have
an impact on cancer.

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.910
I was recruited to
a faculty position.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:12.840
And I thought, wow,
I hadn't thought

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.700
about that, because
that was something

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.630
that women just didn't do,
really, in my generation.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:20.580
When I wanted to go
into work on cancer,

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.370
my friends said, goodbye,
we'll never see you again,

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.140
because you could
bring molecular biology

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.900
to this problem, but it didn't
mean it was going to work,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.360
that you were going
to get anywhere.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.700
The assumption at the
time was, if we understood

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.680
the causes of cancer,
we would surely

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.660
be able to treat the disease.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:39.480
We had a very intense
research group

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:44.730
on the fifth floor of the Center
for Cancer Research at MIT.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:48.000
Oncogenes were discovered, and
the functions of cancer genes

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.370
were discovered, and the
fact that some of these genes

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.250
were active in human
tumors was discovered,

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.550
and how viruses cause
cancer was discovered.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:00.380
And it was one
discovery after another.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:04.260
The legend on the campus was
the lights never went out

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:06.540
on the fifth floor
of the cancer center.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.670
There was always somebody
in the laboratory

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.570
working, 24 hours a day.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:14.970
The MIT Cancer Center ultimately
had four Nobel laureates--

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:20.190
Baltimore, Luria, Susumu
Tonegawa, and then Phil Sharp.

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.470
It was an extraordinary
powerhouse,

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:26.080
and represented the
wave of the future.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:27.940
Luria brings his team in.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.090
They renovate an
old candy factory.

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.970
They're about to get started.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.090
And then there's a crisis.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:39.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:44.390
Scientists, including
many at MIT,

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:49.460
developed technologies for
isolating individual genes,

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:53.390
learning how to take these
genes, put them in cells,

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:56.180
and then make a lot
of the protein that

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:58.830
was coded by the gene.

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:03.000
For the first time,
man had the ability

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.910
to make a biological
organism that

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:08.190
may not have ever
appeared before

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.020
in the biological kingdom.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.030
And that was recombinant DNA.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:15.480
It was an exciting advance
because it really opened up

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:20.460
new ways to be able to
study human genetics.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:24.390
This was new technology
at a world historic level,

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.740
like making steel or
petrochemical industry.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.740
It wasn't just a little
technical advance,

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:32.820
it was going to be a
whole new industry.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.700
This represented the first time
one could splice together genes

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:38.730
from, for example, humans
with the genes of bacteria,

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:42.690
a very unnatural juxtaposition.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:47.820
That led to a lot of debate
about how this should be done.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:49.660
Should it even be done?

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:51.210
What could be the consequences?

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:55.380
What powers would come
with this new technology?

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.590
And this conjured up all
kinds of horrible scenarios

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:00.840
that might spawn
different kinds of plague.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.700
Doctor, you were the one
who got this particular kind

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:07.080
of research started,
and then you

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.150
spoke out about the
possible dangers of it.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:10.920
What happened that
caused you to do that?

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.360
Well, we could
foresee that there

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:14.790
was going to be great
interest in this technique,

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.520
and that many people were
excited about the opportunities

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.225
that was created by it.

00:12:20.225 --> 00:12:22.350
We could also see that some
of the experiments that

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:25.980
might be done might
perhaps create

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.870
some dangerous situation.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:32.100
I started getting telephone
calls calling me a monster

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.180
for actually creating
molecules that could possibly

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.605
be very deleterious to humans.

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:48.740
Any new capability carries
with it a potential danger,

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:55.042
but we weren't actually sure
what the range of danger was.

00:12:55.042 --> 00:12:56.750
In fact, some people
thought there really

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:01.340
wasn't much danger, and
that natural processes

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:03.920
would counter the
synthetic processes

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.440
that we had developed.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.200
So you have all these
other agendas that

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.480
begin to pile on top of this--

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.160
people who are
frightened that this

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:17.810
might have risks to humans,
and other people who

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:20.180
are saying this is
an industrial realm

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:23.580
that we must not surrender.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:27.690
Science had been
moving incredibly fast,

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:32.550
beyond the ability
of the general public

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:37.110
to assess it, to understand
it, become comfortable with it.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:43.140
Many of us felt that we needed
to take some kind of action.

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:45.450
And so the first
thing was a moratorium

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.680
on those experiments.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:51.630
So we called for our scientists
throughout the world to pause,

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.180
to temporarily defer
certain experiments

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.520
until we could meet
and discuss the nature

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:59.220
of the possible hazards and
the kinds of experiments that

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.290
could or should not be done.

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:04.950
The moratorium was remarkably
respected all around the world.

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:08.640
Never in history had a group
of scientists come together

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:14.080
and said, we will not do x.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.360
We called for a conference.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:19.060
And that was the quite
famous Asilomar meeting

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:23.320
that was held in a conference
center in California.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:26.230
That was going to be an
international conclave

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:30.610
to consider the risks and to
try to set forth some principles

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:34.490
for regulating research.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:37.850
There were approximately
150 scientists

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:39.560
from around the world.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:42.980
And we also invited some
lawyers, some ethicists.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:46.600
We invited members of the press.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:49.660
They were every bit as much
involved in the conference

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:52.060
as were the scientists.

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:55.180
Every evening, we
had big beer parties.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:58.870
And so there was people
mixing and talking.

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:00.370
We wanted this to be open.

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:05.820
We didn't want anybody to think
we were some secret cabal.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:09.250
It was an extraordinarily
passionate and even contentious

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:10.630
meeting.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.610
Everybody was sort
of pointing fingers.

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:14.350
It's not my work
that's dangerous.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:16.300
How could that be dangerous?

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:17.980
It's your work that's dangerous.

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:20.110
Don't tell me what
I can do, can't do.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:23.620
So there was a lot of
that back-and-forth.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:25.660
The ethicists and
the lawyers made

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:30.580
it very clear that there were
very severe personal risks.

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.430
Everybody who came of
the so-called experts

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:37.210
thought the risks were very
small, but they're not zero.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:41.530
If it's not zero and there
is some potential risk,

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:45.070
and you can't really
quantify it or identify it,

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.505
the best thing to do is go slow.

00:15:47.505 --> 00:15:48.880
The scientists
stop talking about

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:50.390
if they should do the research.

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:53.280
They start talking about
how to do the research.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.440
In essence, the proposal
was called on the NIH

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:59.200
to develop a series
of guidelines

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:01.570
that would instruct
people on how

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:05.340
to do these experiments safely.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:07.480
The final statement
of that conference

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:10.810
did serve as a basis for the
National Institutes of Health

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:13.780
to promulgate some regulations
of the field, which

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:18.430
they did in late June of 1976.

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:22.270
By and large, recombinant
DNA became doable.

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:25.960
If you followed the guidelines,
there was no problem.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.670
What they hadn't
necessarily counted on

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:30.130
is that individual communities
might become concerned.

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.280
And that's where
Cambridge City Council

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:34.780
says we don't trust the federal
government to make decisions

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:37.197
about work that's being done
within the bounds of the city

00:16:37.197 --> 00:16:38.680
of Cambridge, which
really sets up

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:42.550
one of the most dramatic
cases of scientists

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:45.535
and the concerned public
confronting each other over how

00:16:45.535 --> 00:16:47.660
to regulate this technology
anywhere in the nation.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:55.820
My research involved
recombinant DNA technology.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:59.579
I contributed to that
technology while I was at MIT.

00:16:59.579 --> 00:17:03.410
I had some very interesting
experiments I wanted to do.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:07.550
And then there was a
continuation of the moratorium

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:08.720
here in Cambridge.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.510
But let me assure you that
every city in the United

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:14.119
States of America and
throughout the world

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:20.300
is now discussing the DNA
experimentation in their cities

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.400
and towns and
villages and hamlets.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:27.350
The hall was full of citizens
of Cambridge and scientists

00:17:27.350 --> 00:17:29.480
from MIT and Harvard.

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.370
I'm standing in the
balcony, because the hall

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:36.840
was totally full, watching
Mayor Vellucci here hold forth.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:39.550
Most of us in this
room, including myself,

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:41.710
are lay people.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:45.160
We don't understand
your alphabet.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:47.630
So you will spell
it out for us so

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:50.360
that we know exactly what
you're talking about.

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:53.960
The debate itself came out of
academia originally, really

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:56.990
as questions raised
by some biologists

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:58.940
about the work of
other biologists.

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:00.290
My name is Jonathan King.

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:04.270
I'm associate professor
of biology at MIT.

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:06.770
I have been concerned with this
issue for a number of years.

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:08.810
They essentially went to
city council and said,

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:11.310
we don't know the answers
to these uncertainties,

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:14.720
and we think that it's
a community issue.

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:16.725
It was said that there
was no danger of escape.

00:18:16.725 --> 00:18:18.350
I'd like to go down
on record as saying

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:20.180
that's a patently
false statement.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:23.660
In fact, it is absolutely
certain that they will escape.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:28.160
We wanted regulation, as
existed for many other hazardous

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:30.300
materials in American society.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:34.070
The risks in this case
are purely hypothetical.

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:37.880
Not only has no known dangerous
organism ever been produced,

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:40.010
but I believe it
to be the opinion

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:43.190
of the overwhelming
majority of microbiologists

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:46.940
that there is, in fact, no
significant risk involved

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:50.150
in experiments authorized to be
done by the federal guidelines.

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:52.910
There was a reasonably wide
held belief in Cambridge

00:18:52.910 --> 00:18:56.240
that federal agencies
may not always

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:59.330
have the best interests
of the community in mind.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:04.700
You've got to see it in the
shadow of the atomic bomb,

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:11.180
because that was the singular
event in which the public

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:15.560
suddenly became aware that
science had done things

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:18.050
that they never conceived of.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:22.460
And they kept worrying that
there was an atomic bomb hidden

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:23.630
away in modern biology.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:32.800
The mayor of Cambridge became
quite alarmed by the rhetoric -

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:39.310
visions of viruses and germs
or cloned creatures taking

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:42.790
over the streets or
infecting us or worse.

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:46.270
I have made references
to Frankenstein

00:19:46.270 --> 00:19:48.460
over the past week,
and some people

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:51.370
think this is all a big joke.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:55.720
That was my way of describing
what happens when genes

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:58.750
are put together in a new way.

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:01.480
This is a deadly serious matter.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:04.810
If worse comes to
worse, we could have

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:08.430
a major disaster on our hands.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:09.430
So it was very dramatic.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:15.640
You know, in that
environment, you know,

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:20.350
you say things that, you know,
maybe you wish you hadn't said.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.660
I don't think those
guidelines were written

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:25.030
by a group of people who
represented the public and all

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:25.690
the interests.

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.900
Those guidelines are like having
the tobacco industry write

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.540
guidelines for tobacco safety.

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:38.140
I'm kind of proud of it, even
though many of my colleagues

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:39.190
wouldn't talk to me.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:42.280
This country, this
community has not

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:46.060
decided that we should go
ahead with this research.

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:47.690
It is very dangerous.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:49.000
It's very questionable.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.820
Be it resolved that the
Cambridge City Council insists

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:55.510
that no experimentation
involving recombinant DNA

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:57.430
should be done within
the City of Cambridge

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:59.098
for at least two years.

00:20:59.098 --> 00:21:01.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:03.580
[BOOS]

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:05.950
Here at the moment,
we could unleash

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:10.780
a whole new powerful tool
to answer our questions.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:11.280
No.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:14.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:17.320
Had there been
something that happened,

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:20.410
an organism created
that disseminated itself

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:22.720
through Cambridge
and maybe the world,

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:25.810
we would have been
blamed for that,

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.150
and it would have brought
a halt to the direction

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:30.640
of modern biology,
modern science.

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:33.340
So there was a tremendous
amount at stake.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:34.885
Powerful forces were at play.

00:21:34.885 --> 00:21:37.390
The venture capitalists
were already there

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:40.290
in the background
watching and waiting.

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:45.700
The mayor appointed a
commission of ordinary citizens

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:49.990
to answer one simple
question-- are these scientists

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:51.370
being honest with us?

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:54.880
Is the process that was
put in place at Asilomar

00:21:54.880 --> 00:22:00.530
a process that is going to
safeguard the community?

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:05.390
That was a novel reality
for the academic community.

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:09.980
They were not accustomed to
citizens perceiving themselves

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:14.690
as equal partners in the
development of science.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:16.700
In the end, they decided
that they thought

00:22:16.700 --> 00:22:20.240
the processes that were being
developed at the National

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:22.850
Institutes of Health
were appropriate

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:27.440
and that this technology
was the future of biology

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:30.830
and should be freed.

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:37.280
It took about a year to go from
the first city hall meeting

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:39.830
to the position in
which we could really

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:44.690
begin to plan to do those
experiments in Cambridge.

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:47.420
The ban on using
recombinant DNA was lifted,

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:49.663
and that resulted
in an explosion

00:22:49.663 --> 00:22:51.080
of biomedical
research, because it

00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:55.040
was such a useful
technological innovation.

00:22:55.040 --> 00:23:00.140
The then-mayor of Cambridge,
in a reaction to this activism,

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:03.740
led the creation of the
nation's very first zoning

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:07.280
rules for a biotech-related
research development,

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:11.390
leading this as a way of
controlling the growth

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.580
of biotech in Cambridge.

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:16.310
In fact, it had the
opposite result,

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:18.230
which was creating
predictability

00:23:18.230 --> 00:23:19.520
for the industry.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:21.470
Where there was
no predictability

00:23:21.470 --> 00:23:24.320
in zoning in development across
the United States of America,

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:25.490
there was in Cambridge.

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.400
There was in Kendall Square.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:29.240
And that's one of the
reasons why Cambridge

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:30.860
got out ahead of so
many other areas,

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:34.100
because we had a
blueprint that set out

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:36.590
what could be done in the city.

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:40.100
It liberated Harvard
and MIT to move forward

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:44.860
and to become centers
of the new biology.

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:47.410
When Biogen came to
Cambridge and applied

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:51.040
for the first
recombinant DNA license

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:54.220
in a commercial
organization in Cambridge,

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:58.060
Cambridge said, we've
gone through this debate.

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:01.780
People in Cambridge know
what the issues are.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:03.730
We have these guidelines.

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:05.390
If you adhere to
these guidelines,

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:07.700
like the universities,
we're fine.

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:11.890
And Mayor Vellucci showed up
for the opening of the Biogen's

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:16.660
first laboratory in Cambridge
and cut the ribbon and said,

00:24:16.660 --> 00:24:19.780
you know, I like
recombinant DNA experiments,

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:21.790
particularly when
they pay taxes.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:23.530
And that led to human insulin.

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:25.090
That led to human
growth hormone.

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:28.600
That led to interferons
for infectious disease

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:32.830
and multiple sclerosis, and it's
led to treatments of cancer.

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:37.160
And it's just an extraordinary,
extraordinary breakthrough.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:41.380
So what we had, then,
were a few small companies

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:47.520
that really provided the
nucleus for a biotech industry

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:49.480
in Kendall Square.

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:51.820
The early days,
people were guessing

00:24:51.820 --> 00:24:54.400
as to whether it would
take 10 years or 2 years

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:57.370
to be able to make a particular
protein in pure form,

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:01.000
and the FDA didn't even have
guidelines as to how they

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:03.340
would regulate new products.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:05.260
And those guidelines
kept changing,

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:07.905
and so it was kind of a
real wild west moment.

00:25:07.905 --> 00:25:09.530
It was really the
birth of an industry.

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:14.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.920
From these roots
in recombinant DNA,

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.800
a lot of interesting
things happened.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:22.590
One is the cures.

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:25.050
For a long time,
the industry was

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:30.240
very hesitant about
using the "cure" word,

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:33.840
but we've gotten to the point
where if we're not eradicating,

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:36.840
we're substantially and
dramatically improving

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:39.480
the lives of individuals
with rare disease.

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:43.780
So that's a real
visceral impact.

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:45.970
I began working on the
intellectual property,

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:49.330
the patents mostly, for
the fledgling Biogen, who,

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:51.250
at that time, had no employees.

00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:53.590
They were basically
seven or eight scientists

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:56.260
in their labs around
Europe and the US.

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:59.900
Biogen started by two
venture capitalists.

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:02.170
They had a fantastic idea.

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:05.380
Get the best scientists
from all over the world

00:26:05.380 --> 00:26:07.480
and start a company.

00:26:07.480 --> 00:26:10.240
A biotechnology firm that
starts could probably

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:12.790
expect to take 10 years before
its product came to market.

00:26:12.790 --> 00:26:14.500
That's 10 years
before any profit,

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:16.792
and it's 10 years of needing
an immense amount of money

00:26:16.792 --> 00:26:18.640
to sustain this operation.

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:20.560
And the probability of
success, historically,

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:24.220
has been about 5%, so when you
have these incredibly risky

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:25.720
investments, you're
going to have

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:27.137
a challenge in
being able to raise

00:26:27.137 --> 00:26:29.980
the proper kind of
financing to fund them.

00:26:29.980 --> 00:26:33.610
Biotech was the
first example where

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:38.020
Wall Street was willing
to fund new technology

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:41.320
over a long period of
not being profitable

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:45.400
to own part of the company
when it became profitable.

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:47.230
What really put
Biogen on the map

00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:49.030
was the cloning of interferon.

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:54.140
It really was this holy
grail of biotechnology.

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:59.140
Interferon is a natural compound
produced by the body when

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:01.540
it is infected by a virus.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:04.060
It seemed to kill
viruses, kill cancers.

00:27:04.060 --> 00:27:05.043
It was amazing.

00:27:05.043 --> 00:27:06.460
But you couldn't
get enough of it.

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:08.500
And because it was
so unavailable,

00:27:08.500 --> 00:27:12.130
there grew up a bunch of
myths about how potent

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:16.570
it was going to be, and it's
going to be a cure for cancer.

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.580
And Biogen set that as
one of their projects

00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:21.130
to try to clone
it and produce it.

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:23.830
We came to an agreement
with Charles Weissman,

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:26.110
one of the founders
of molecular biology,

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:29.560
that he would develop a
research program to clone

00:27:29.560 --> 00:27:32.560
the gene for alpha interferon.

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:35.320
We didn't have a
lab, and those of us

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:39.520
who undertook projects undertook
to do them in their university

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:42.100
labs.

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:47.500
On Christmas Day, 1979,
Charles is up in Davos,

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:50.740
where he skis over the
holidays with friends,

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:56.010
and back in his lab is
Nagata, who's a postdoc.

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:59.920
Nagata was extremely
hard-working,

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:02.410
and within a few
months, he was then

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:07.990
successful in actually cloning
the cDNA of interferon alpha.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:12.550
Nagata calls Charles
and says he thinks

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:16.720
he's seen a positive out of one
or two of the bacteria colonies

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:18.730
he was assaying.

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:23.140
When Charles cloned and
expressed interferon,

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:27.250
it was a world-shaking
event, and we thought

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:31.010
it will cure everything.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:32.410
I flew to Switzerland
immediately

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:35.200
and spent the next two weeks
writing the patent application,

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:36.760
because we knew we're in a race.

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:38.320
We knew other
companies were trying

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:40.180
to clone interferon as well.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:42.700
We, as a scientific
advisory board,

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:47.290
had talked the company into
having our January meeting

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:48.745
in the Caribbean.

00:28:48.745 --> 00:28:51.190
And the first day
of the meeting,

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:55.870
Charles gets up and
explains what he's done

00:28:55.870 --> 00:28:59.140
and the evidence that
this is alpha interferon

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:00.740
that he has the gene for.

00:29:00.740 --> 00:29:04.780
And we were just astounded.

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:07.990
The board, at the time,
wanted to publicize this,

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:11.650
because it would boost
the share price of Biogen.

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:14.890
And I didn't want to
have this publicized

00:29:14.890 --> 00:29:19.990
before I had a scientific
paper describing these results.

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:24.340
He agreed to hold
the press conference,

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:28.900
if he had a chance to present
the data to scientists, who

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:31.250
could judge it and critique it.

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:35.560
And I said, we can get a
group of scientists at MIT

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:38.830
for a seminar on Monday.

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:41.380
And so that became the plan.

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:42.110
We flew off.

00:29:42.110 --> 00:29:46.420
Charles has got a typewriter,
which he bought in the hotel.

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:48.385
And he's pounding
out this manuscript

00:29:48.385 --> 00:29:50.830
while he's flying,
because he wants

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:53.800
to be able to give people
a description of what

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:55.390
the data is.

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:57.970
And Charles came to
MIT, gave a seminar,

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:00.760
and then gave a
press conference.

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:05.260
Charles gets up and starts
talking about cloning the gene,

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:10.090
and it's all DNA and joining
and putting in bacteria

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:11.823
and assaying interferon.

00:30:11.823 --> 00:30:13.990
I thought it was going to
be a big event-- you know,

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:16.000
cloning interferon
is something people

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.040
had been trying
to do for so long.

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:21.320
But what ended up was there were
a lot of critical questions,

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:24.640
you know, what is an
academic scientist doing

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:26.510
working for a company?

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:29.140
And the reporters aren't
getting it, so I get up,

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:30.970
and I take the
microphone from Charles,

00:30:30.970 --> 00:30:33.970
and I tell the reporters why
cloning alpha interferon is

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:34.720
a great thing.

00:30:37.013 --> 00:30:39.430
Then the next day on the front
page of the New York Times,

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:41.770
it was, you know,
Biogen cures cancer.

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:44.410
And so that really
set Biogen on the map

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:47.020
and got people really thinking
about the possibilities

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:48.160
of recombinant DNA.

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:49.930
Scientists in
Boston today claimed

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:51.310
a major medical breakthrough.

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:53.320
They said they
have used bacteria

00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:56.230
to produce a substance
called interferon.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:59.200
Interferon fights many viruses,
including those believed

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:01.330
to cause many forms of cancer.

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:03.490
It was, at the
time, quite unusual

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:10.020
that a biologist would be linked
to some commercial interest.

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:12.780
There was the ethic
that applying one's work

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:16.380
to common everyday uses
in clinical applications

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:21.240
was dirty, would somehow sully
the academic basic biomedical

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:22.290
research agenda.

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:25.470
Some of my colleagues
thought it was an awful thing

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:27.460
to have done, you know.

00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:31.080
They actually called me
names for having succumbed

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:36.470
to this financial influence.

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:38.240
But MIT was different.

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:41.430
It derived from a
decades-long ethos

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:45.030
at MIT, where one had
encouraged electrical engineers

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:48.390
and computer engineers to
apply what they had learned

00:31:48.390 --> 00:31:52.560
to everyday practicalities that
yielded many kinds of benefit

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:55.280
for humanity at large.

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:58.910
The concept of, should
a university be working

00:31:58.910 --> 00:32:03.650
with industry, will the grubby
fingerprints of industry soil

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:06.260
the ivory tower of academia--

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:09.410
MIT didn't have to go through
that, because they weren't

00:32:09.410 --> 00:32:12.340
set up as an ivory tower.

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:17.330
MIT started off with the
charter of bringing science

00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:19.470
to industry and agriculture.

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:22.400
So the faculty were
working with industry

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:27.080
probably since MIT
opened its doors in 1865.

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:29.660
They were starting
companies all along.

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:35.950
Interferon doesn't really have
much effect on lots of cancers,

00:32:35.950 --> 00:32:37.500
but it does have
an effect, and it

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:39.870
became a more than a
billion dollar a year drug.

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:42.570
It was sold for a
particular type of leukemia,

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:45.990
but more importantly, for
hepatitis C. And for years,

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:49.290
it was the only, really,
treatment for hepatitis C.

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:52.230
After this discovery,
scientists all around the world

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:54.060
who were working
in the field began

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:56.070
to think about the
commercial aspects of it

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:58.980
rather than just the
academic aspects of it.

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:02.100
In a sense, the entire
industry starts from one or two

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:02.760
companies--

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:05.696
Genentech on the west
coast, Biogen here.

00:33:05.696 --> 00:33:09.630
You know, there are now
2,000, 3,000 companies.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.240
It's infection.

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:14.310
The growth of the industry, in
large part, is by infection.

00:33:14.310 --> 00:33:17.310
People see it's possible.

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:19.560
It was not planned.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:23.160
Most of the biotech
companies, virtually all,

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:27.450
were started by
faculty entrepreneurs.

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:31.560
The company that I
helped start, Genzyme,

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:35.640
focused on making
recombinant enzymes that

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:39.180
could be used to treat
certain genetic disorders,

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:42.180
such as Gaucher disease.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:44.640
I have seven grandchildren,
and one of them

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:48.400
has Gaucher and has been
treated with the drug

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:50.640
his grandfather helped develop.

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:52.440
My name is Andrew Steinert.

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:56.070
I'm 16 years old, and I
attend Belmont High School.

00:33:56.070 --> 00:34:01.440
Gaucher disease is a disease in
which the body cannot degrade

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:05.429
a particular glycolipid, a
particular fatty substance.

00:34:05.429 --> 00:34:07.139
It affects the
liver and the spleen

00:34:07.139 --> 00:34:09.840
and the bones and
things like that.

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:12.300
He used to have to
go to the hospital

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:15.150
every three weeks
to get an infusion.

00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:18.719
He now gets it at home,
and he simply sits and does

00:34:18.719 --> 00:34:23.280
his homework while they put
the line in and transfuse

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:26.710
in the medicine.

00:34:26.710 --> 00:34:29.590
Every ethnic group has
its own genetic diseases,

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:33.940
because one person
develops a genetic disease,

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:38.949
and then unknowingly, various
descendants of that person

00:34:38.949 --> 00:34:42.780
have a child together, and you
get two bad copies of the gene,

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:44.560
and you have a disease.

00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:46.000
That's what happened
with Gaucher.

00:34:48.590 --> 00:34:51.830
We were very fortunate to have
the Genzyme drug in place,

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:53.330
clearly.

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:59.090
And it also started me working
on trying to develop solutions

00:34:59.090 --> 00:35:02.180
for other rare diseases.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:06.950
Biotechnology can enable him
to leave a really symptom-free,

00:35:06.950 --> 00:35:11.240
carefree life despite
having Gaucher's.

00:35:11.240 --> 00:35:12.400
He's never been sick.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:16.190
He's very healthy,
very energetic.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:18.620
This summer, I plan to
bike across the country

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:21.030
from Savannah, Georgia
to Los Angeles,

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:22.700
California over six weeks.

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:24.740
It's about 85 miles a day.

00:35:24.740 --> 00:35:27.200
It sounds crazy, but I
look forward to doing it.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:28.640
[CHEERING]

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:30.860
The exciting thing
is once you make

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:35.430
an advance of that
nature in medicine,

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:37.670
it's an advance forever.

00:35:37.670 --> 00:35:42.510
It's that culture, the culture
of faculty entrepreneurs,

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:43.760
that's the key.

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:46.160
And that's what really
drove Kendall Square.

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:53.300
Kendall Square
actually has a history

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:57.410
that dates back into the
post-industrial revolution.

00:35:57.410 --> 00:36:00.740
It was once a geography
that was deeply involved

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:02.720
in manufacturing.

00:36:02.720 --> 00:36:05.630
In the 1950s and 1960s,
New England as a whole

00:36:05.630 --> 00:36:07.472
experiences an industrial slump.

00:36:07.472 --> 00:36:09.430
Many of the businesses
that made East Cambridge

00:36:09.430 --> 00:36:12.880
such a vibrant neighborhood
either close or relocate.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:14.960
What was left behind
in Kendall Square

00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:20.030
was a barren wasteland of
former manufacturing facilities.

00:36:20.030 --> 00:36:23.690
NASA was in a very expansive
mood in this early period.

00:36:23.690 --> 00:36:26.630
It had just been
created in 1958,

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:29.230
and building some
new research centers.

00:36:29.230 --> 00:36:31.580
And one of those that
they felt was essential

00:36:31.580 --> 00:36:35.750
was a research center
to study electronics.

00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:39.620
It was proposed that
Cambridge be the site.

00:36:39.620 --> 00:36:42.800
There were over 100
takings by the government

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:47.270
to consolidate parcels,
raze them, and prepare them

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:49.610
for a competitive bid
that Massachusetts

00:36:49.610 --> 00:36:54.070
was in the middle of, which
was for the location of NASA.

00:36:54.070 --> 00:36:55.940
People thought for
many years that this

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:58.850
is what the future
of Cambridge would

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:03.440
look like-- a hub of aerospace,
research, and technology

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:06.090
for years to come.

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:09.050
But then that plan
never comes to fruition.

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:11.720
President Richard
Nixon, concerned

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:15.410
by the deficits and the costs
associated with the Vietnam

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:20.840
War, decided to make significant
cuts in the national budget.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:25.990
Among those cuts was the
electronics research center.

00:37:25.990 --> 00:37:31.550
It left behind the cleared
land and a lot of parking lots.

00:37:31.550 --> 00:37:35.810
By the late 1970s, MIT is not
only sitting inside of a city,

00:37:35.810 --> 00:37:38.690
but it's also sitting next
to an area of land that

00:37:38.690 --> 00:37:41.000
is effectively vacant.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:44.660
And that is the land where a lot
of the first biotech companies

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:47.300
that want to locate
next to MIT move into,

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:48.560
and they find office space.

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:51.230
They find buildings
that have strong floors

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:53.570
and high ceilings, so they
can sort of do flexible lab

00:37:53.570 --> 00:37:55.380
configurations in.

00:37:55.380 --> 00:37:59.030
And we just got lucky
here in many respects.

00:37:59.030 --> 00:38:02.030
If they hadn't torn down
all those factories,

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:04.310
if the land hadn't
been vacant, would it

00:38:04.310 --> 00:38:06.610
have developed the same way?

00:38:06.610 --> 00:38:09.480
At the time, people thought
that was an enormous loss.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.830
But in retrospect, it was a
silver lining behind a cloud,

00:38:12.830 --> 00:38:14.900
because it made an
awful lot of space

00:38:14.900 --> 00:38:17.780
available in the
subsequent decades

00:38:17.780 --> 00:38:19.940
for the building
of, for example,

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:22.460
the Whitehead Institute.

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:25.730
Jack Whitehead had
developed a company

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:29.300
to make scientific
equipment, and so he

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:33.770
wanted to build a
research institute that

00:38:33.770 --> 00:38:40.380
would be his gift to
the research community.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:42.060
There were people
who were suspicious

00:38:42.060 --> 00:38:43.560
that the White
Institute was somehow

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:46.710
a vehicle for enabling
Jack Whitehead to fund

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:50.240
his own biotech
investment ventures.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:53.120
This is a business
guy, an industrialist

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:55.650
who made a fortune
out of innovation.

00:38:55.650 --> 00:39:00.050
He's going to look to skim off
all the innovation that comes

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:02.750
out of this research institute.

00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:04.790
The Boston Globe
published a series

00:39:04.790 --> 00:39:10.170
of articles that sort of
inflamed the Boston area.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:13.940
This was a bit of out of
control paranoia, and, in fact,

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:16.750
Whitehead was accused
of all manner of things.

00:39:16.750 --> 00:39:19.310
Here he was trying to
do something totally

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:22.010
philanthropic, and he
couldn't convince people

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:24.260
that it was totally
philanthropic.

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:28.910
Academic Council discussed
it, and then voted it down,

00:39:28.910 --> 00:39:33.590
and they voted it down
by a huge margin, by 85%.

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:36.470
As Whitehead observed
later, making money

00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:40.370
was much easier
than giving it away.

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:44.270
He was giving us the opportunity
to move a little closer

00:39:44.270 --> 00:39:46.920
to medicine without
having a medical school.

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:49.910
And so I had to convince
my colleagues that that's

00:39:49.910 --> 00:39:53.450
what was going to happen, and
not what the Boston Globe said

00:39:53.450 --> 00:39:54.680
was going to happen.

00:39:54.680 --> 00:39:57.410
And after it had
had a full hearing

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:01.750
for a year and a half of
discussion on the ground,

00:40:01.750 --> 00:40:04.760
it then went back
to Academic Council

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:09.520
and was voted in
approval by 85%.

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:11.630
And in the end, we
got a piece of land

00:40:11.630 --> 00:40:16.030
right next to the MIT
campus, and we built it.

00:40:16.030 --> 00:40:19.780
We were the pioneer
building there, yes.

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:24.910
By 1990, the Whitehead Institute
is perceived and acknowledged

00:40:24.910 --> 00:40:27.190
as, perhaps, the
world's leading research

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:28.630
center in molecular biology.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:30.580
It was that fast.

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:32.590
The Whitehead
Institute's excellence

00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:34.480
is due to the vision
of David Baltimore.

00:40:34.480 --> 00:40:37.540
He could sense talent, and
when people like Eric Lander

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:41.990
came along, he saw Lander to
be a very intelligent person.

00:40:41.990 --> 00:40:46.120
Here was a guy who had no
formal training in genetics,

00:40:46.120 --> 00:40:48.080
in biology, in medicine.

00:40:48.080 --> 00:40:49.700
He was not really a biologist.

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:51.790
He was a mathematician.

00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:54.250
And Eric decided
that he was going

00:40:54.250 --> 00:40:57.760
to leave behind much of
his mathematical career

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:01.300
and his business career and
become a geneticist just

00:41:01.300 --> 00:41:04.690
at the time when there were the
first discussions of sequencing

00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:06.640
the human genome.

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:09.670
This heady idea that
when we could barely

00:41:09.670 --> 00:41:11.980
sequence a couple
hundred letters of DNA

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:14.860
after a long day's
work, we might read out

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:18.850
the entire human genetic
code captured the imagination

00:41:18.850 --> 00:41:23.290
of a young new generation
of which I was part.

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:24.925
And it just changed
all our lives.

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:31.330
Very quickly, we formed
the Whitehead MIT Center

00:41:31.330 --> 00:41:33.040
for Genome Research.

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:37.180
That becomes one of
the key enterprises

00:41:37.180 --> 00:41:41.880
in the larger human
genome sequencing project.

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:44.565
Somewhere in the neighborhood
of 1/3 of the human genome

00:41:44.565 --> 00:41:48.240
was sequenced here at MIT.

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:51.540
Suddenly, it meant instead
of wandering forever

00:41:51.540 --> 00:41:55.050
trying to look for one gene
that's causing a disease,

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:58.290
you had an inventory of
all 20,000 human genes.

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.450
You could see their locations.

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.730
You could map their inheritance.

00:42:02.730 --> 00:42:05.010
You could begin to
collect not just

00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:07.950
one genome, but by
using it as a scaffold,

00:42:07.950 --> 00:42:10.780
all the variation in
the human population.

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:12.660
It sort of turned on the lights.

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.470
We could look around, and
we could see everything.

00:42:15.470 --> 00:42:17.430
That genome center
became a magnet

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:21.410
for smart, young,
PhDs and MDs who

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:24.150
all knew that this was an
important part of the future

00:42:24.150 --> 00:42:26.310
of biology, and thus
was born the idea

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:28.610
of creating the Broad Institute.

00:42:28.610 --> 00:42:31.350
And as a result of all
this, what ended up

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:34.380
within a radius of 150 yards--

00:42:34.380 --> 00:42:37.020
four extraordinary
institutions--

00:42:37.020 --> 00:42:39.960
the core MIT Biology
Department, the Koch Institute,

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:42.600
the Broad Institute, and
the Whitehead Institute.

00:42:42.600 --> 00:42:45.990
And, of course, up the street is
the McGovern Institute focused

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:48.050
essentially on the brain
and nervous system,

00:42:48.050 --> 00:42:50.370
and also the Picower Institute.

00:42:50.370 --> 00:42:54.330
All of these benefit, I think,
from the initial gamble that

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:57.130
was taken with the Whitehead.

00:42:57.130 --> 00:42:59.370
In the 20th century,
we treated diseases

00:42:59.370 --> 00:43:01.390
based on their symptoms.

00:43:01.390 --> 00:43:03.210
What's happened in
the 21st century

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:06.660
is all of medicine
is getting rethought

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:09.960
in terms of the fundamental
causes of disease--

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:14.040
the genes that are mutated, the
specific cellular mechanisms

00:43:14.040 --> 00:43:15.600
that are deranged.

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:18.750
And it means that
targeted therapies

00:43:18.750 --> 00:43:21.990
based on a real
mechanistic understanding

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:23.400
have become the norm.

00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:26.130
And because of that,
hundreds and hundreds

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:28.590
of new kinds of drugs
are being developed.

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:32.450
Thousands and thousands of
clinical trials are being run.

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:36.180
And we're beginning to see
truly remarkable responses.

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:38.610
