00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:24.300
That's because everybody
wants to be here.

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:26.460
LabCentral is a really
interesting innovation

00:46:26.460 --> 00:46:27.720
in and of itself.

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:30.600
There was a recognition that
these tech incubators are

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:33.930
fantastic, but what the
life sciences really needs

00:46:33.930 --> 00:46:38.550
is wet lab incubators, a
completely different animal.

00:46:38.550 --> 00:46:41.340
There's often what's referred
to as the valley of death

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:44.530
between research in a university
and a successful company.

00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:46.530
And the reason is the
cost of building a lab can

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:47.680
be millions of dollars.

00:46:47.680 --> 00:46:51.090
So what we discovered was that
if we shared the resources,

00:46:51.090 --> 00:46:53.310
and you brought all the
entrepreneurs together,

00:46:53.310 --> 00:46:55.800
not only could they get
a very efficient access

00:46:55.800 --> 00:46:58.170
to the physical infrastructure
that they needed

00:46:58.170 --> 00:47:02.460
to kind of get through that
valley of death at a lower cost

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:04.623
and hopefully not
die, but they also

00:47:04.623 --> 00:47:06.540
found that they were
around other people doing

00:47:06.540 --> 00:47:08.040
the same thing.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:10.350
The people that traverse
the valley of death

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:14.490
are the ones that sort of,
like, hold hands and get across

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:16.992
through advice or connections.

00:47:16.992 --> 00:47:18.450
That's actually
what Kendall Square

00:47:18.450 --> 00:47:20.460
is really good at is
sort of like holding

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:23.670
hands across that valley.

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:26.100
The vast majority
of biotech companies

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:29.550
never are profitable from
selling any kind of drugs.

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:30.780
That's not their goal.

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:33.750
Their goal is to take a piece of
intellectual property, a patent

00:47:33.750 --> 00:47:36.150
or an idea for a new
therapy, and develop it

00:47:36.150 --> 00:47:38.880
to the point where a big
pharma company will look at it

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:40.350
and say, gee, this has promise.

00:47:40.350 --> 00:47:43.230
We will buy it up or
license the technology,

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:45.600
and we will take it through
all the way to approval.

00:47:45.600 --> 00:47:50.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:54.180
Today is our Amgen Pitch It
for a Golden Ticket Event.

00:47:54.180 --> 00:47:56.580
Once a year, we
award two companies

00:47:56.580 --> 00:47:59.910
a golden ticket which gives them
free bench space at LabCentral

00:47:59.910 --> 00:48:00.590
for one year.

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:03.510
And so we had 44
companies applied.

00:48:03.510 --> 00:48:05.970
We selected six to do the
pitch, and we'll actually

00:48:05.970 --> 00:48:08.460
hand out the two winners of
the golden ticket this evening.

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:12.180
We are standing on the
quite literal precipice

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:14.910
of a true revolution in
the way we treat cancer.

00:48:14.910 --> 00:48:18.180
Our first product is a
biodegradable minimally

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:21.240
invasive implantable
patch that we can embed

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:23.860
with the powerful toxic agents.

00:48:23.860 --> 00:48:25.410
These onco-selective
messenger RNAs

00:48:25.410 --> 00:48:27.870
are delivered into cancer
cells and healthy cells

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:30.510
with lipid nanoparticles,
just like a cancer vaccine.

00:48:30.510 --> 00:48:32.880
Just giving these guys the
first step on the ladder

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:33.720
is very critical.

00:48:33.720 --> 00:48:35.820
Sometimes that can
be the hardest part.

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:39.180
It's a non-viral
transfection technology

00:48:39.180 --> 00:48:42.946
in which cells are flowing
through a microchannel.

00:48:42.946 --> 00:48:43.446
